# PART 6 — ZERO-AI LOCAL DOCUMENT PROCESSING

## 6.1 Local Processing Architecture

Implement the following pipeline:

UPLOAD
→ FILE VALIDATION
→ PRIVATE STORAGE
→ DOCUMENT CLASSIFICATION
→ DIGITAL PDF TEXT EXTRACTION
→ OCR FALLBACK
→ DOCUMENT-SPECIFIC PARSING
→ FIELD NORMALIZATION
→ CROSS-DOCUMENT VALIDATION
→ USER REVIEW
→ DATABASE PERSISTENCE

## 6.2 Recommended Local Technologies

Inspect compatibility with the existing project before adding dependencies.

Potential libraries:

- `pdf-parse` for digital PDF text extraction.
- `tesseract.js` for local OCR.
- Poppler utilities for rendering scanned PDF pages into images.
- An image processing library such as Sharp for grayscale, rotation and contrast preprocessing.
- Regex for deterministic pattern extraction.
- TypeScript schemas for validation.

All OCR language models and required runtime assets must be installed locally.

No OCR component may silently download external processing assets during production execution.

Verify offline operation.

## 6.3 Digital PDF and Scanned PDF Handling

Digital PDF:
- Extract embedded text.
- Preserve page-level text information where supported.
- Run document-specific parsing rules.

Scanned PDF:
- Render each page locally.
- Preprocess the image.
- Run Tesseract OCR.
- Retain page references.
- Apply parsing rules.

Images:
- Apply local preprocessing.
- Run OCR.
- Apply field extraction rules.

Low-quality documents:
- Mark extraction as unsuccessful or uncertain.
- Allow manual review.
- Never invent a value.

OCR confidence scores are not proof that a financial value is correct.

