# PART 7 — PAYMENT AND REALISATION MANAGEMENT

Payment management must be a complete relational workflow.

A single invoice may receive multiple partial payments.

A single inward remittance may also need allocation across multiple invoices.

Therefore, do not rely on one payment field per shipment.

Implement normalized entities:

- Shipment / Export Invoice.
- Payment / Inward Remittance.
- Payment Allocation.
- Bank.
- Bank Charges.
- Realisation Details.

Each payment must support:

- IRM number.
- Bank.
- Remittance date.
- Credit date.
- Foreign currency.
- Foreign currency amount.
- INR amount.
- Exchange rate.
- Bank charges where documented.
- Supporting FIRC document.
- Payment allocation.

Calculate:

Total Allocated USD = Sum of valid payment allocations.

Outstanding USD = Invoice USD − Total Allocated USD.

Excess USD = Allocated USD − Invoice USD, when positive.

Support partial payment status.

Do not mark an invoice fully paid solely because an INR amount exists.

Enforce valid allocation limits and allow authorized handling of overpayments through explicit business rules.

## 7.1 Forex Gain/Loss

The legacy workbook uses:

AB = Received INR − FOB INR.

This can be misleading for partial payments.

Do not reproduce this formula as actual realized forex.

Calculate realized forex at payment-allocation level using an approved accounting basis.

A possible formula is:

Realised Forex = Allocated USD × (Realisation Rate − Approved Booking Rate)

However, confirm the appropriate accounting basis with the business/accountant before making this the authoritative figure.

Separate:

- Unpaid principal.
- Realised forex.
- Bank charges.
- Other adjustments.

Provide a transparent calculation breakdown.

